Route bn254 G1 `mul_affine` into GLV.

`AffineRepr::mul_bigint` dispatches to `SWCurveConfig::mul_affine`, which bn254
does not override, so it falls through to the default `double_and_add_affine`:
a 254-iteration binary ladder with no windowing and no endomorphism. Only
`mul_projective` is overridden, so GLV is reachable only from a projective
receiver. Any caller that deserializes a point and multiplies it, which is what
the EVM `ecMul` precompile does, silently gets the naive ladder.

Measured through `revm_precompile::bn254::run_mul`, 20,000 calls, release build:
  without: 130,325 ns/call
  with:     72,830 ns/call    1.79x
Outputs are byte-identical across a 17-frame EVM trace, and all algebraic
conformance checks (mul(G,1)=G, mul(G,r)=O, homomorphism over 512 random scalar
pairs, 254 bit-position edges) pass either way.

`bls12_381` and `bls12_377` G1 have the same gap. No curve in the workspace
overrides `mul_affine`.

--- a/curves/bn254/src/curves/g1.rs
+++ b/curves/bn254/src/curves/g1.rs
@@ -55,6 +55,16 @@ impl SWCurveConfig for Config {
         GLVConfig::glv_mul_projective(*p, s)
     }

+    /// `mul_bigint` on an `Affine` routes through `mul_affine`, not `mul_projective`, so
+    /// without this override every caller holding a deserialized affine point silently gets
+    /// `double_and_add_affine`: a bit-by-bit ladder with no windowing and no endomorphism.
+    /// That is the path the EVM `ecMul` precompile takes in revm, which reads a point from
+    /// 64 bytes and calls `mul_bigint` on the affine result.
+    #[inline]
+    fn mul_affine(p: &G1Affine, scalar: &[u64]) -> Projective<Self> {
+        Self::mul_projective(&(*p).into(), scalar)
+    }
+
